## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Filed from lane-enum's B100 report, 2026-08-31, measured not reasoned.

## Measured on a MASTER build, both sides, both positions

  * `double MakeDouble()` and `float MakeFloat()` bind an `int()` slot --
    SILENT, at the argument position AND at `IntMaker@ d = MakeDouble;`
  * `void TakeDouble(double)` binds a `void(int)` slot -- SILENT, same two
    positions
  * CONTROL in the same probe: `string()` at an `int()` slot IS rejected, so
    the check is running rather than absent

## Why it is wrong

`funcdef_types_compatible`'s `(Primitive, Primitive) => a == b ||
is_convertible(...)` treats a function pointer's parameter and return types as
if they were VALUES being converted. They are not. The engine cannot insert a
numeric conversion into a delegate's call path -- the delegate is called with
the caller's types directly -- so a `double()` cannot stand in for an `int()`.

## Why it is worth doing properly

One predicate, reached by B023, B076, B089, B102, B091 and B100 alike. Fixing
it once fixes every funcdef position; getting it wrong breaks all six. That is
the good version of the "a rule living at one of N sites" shape this repo keeps
hitting -- here the rule is already at ONE site, so keep it that way.

## The risk polarity, and it is the tight one

This TIGHTENS a check, so every change is a candidate false ERROR on code that
compiles today. Before landing, sweep the fleet and confirm the delta is either
zero or explicable line by line. A non-zero delta here is not automatically a
win -- it may be plugins that legitimately compile.

Note the value-conversion rules elsewhere are NOT wrong and must not be
disturbed: `int x = someDouble;` is a real conversion. The claim is narrowly
that a funcdef SIGNATURE match is not a conversion site.

## Unmeasurable half, state it rather than guess

Whether the engine rejects `double()` at an `int()` slot is REASONED from how
delegates are called, not probed -- B097 blocks probing. That reasoning is
strong (a delegate has no conversion seam) but it is reasoning, and the ticket
should say so where it lands, as B102 did for const-ness. If the sweep shows
real plugins relying on the current leniency, that is evidence AGAINST the
reasoning and should stop the change rather than be worked around.

## Tests

Generalise per repo CLAUDE.md across the primitive lattice in both directions
(int/uint/int64/float/double, widening and narrowing), for the RETURN type and
each PARAMETER, at every position the predicate is reached from -- argument,
declaration initializer, assignment, and the B102 template-member slot. Include
exact-match positive controls for each, or a predicate that rejects everything
scores as a pass.

## Related

B100 (where it was found), B027 (parameter MODE comparison, the sibling axis --
do not duplicate it), B102 (const-ness peeled deliberately; check the two do
